course: dictionary
lesson: 2784
title: Dishonesty and trust
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dishonesty is a noun. It names behaviour that is not honest, such as lying, cheating or stealing. It also names the quality of being someone who is not honest. Here is one context example: Proverbs 16:28 says, «A gossip separates close friends». Passing on what you were told in trust is a kind of dishonesty, because it breaks that trust.
explain: Look at the clue words around the new word. In «an act of dishonesty», the word act tells you dishonesty is something a person does, not a feeling. In «accused of dishonesty», the word accused tells you someone is being blamed for doing something wrong. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then test your meaning in the sentence.
explain: Honesty is a key value, so dishonesty is what happens when that value is broken. The Ten Commandments say «You shall not steal» and «You shall not bear false witness». Both are rules about honesty and trust, and breaking them is dishonesty.
fact: Dishonesty means behaviour that is not honest, such as lying, cheating or stealing.
fact: Dishonesty also means the quality of being a person who is not honest.
fact: Dishonesty is uncountable, so we say «dishonesty», not «dishonesties».
fact: «Accused of dishonesty» means blamed for being dishonest.
fact: Dishonesty breaks trust, and trust is hard to win back once it is lost.
exercise: Mia was accused of dishonesty after she copied her friend's homework. What does accused of dishonesty mean? | Asked to help the teacher | *Blamed for being dishonest | Praised for being clever | Given a prize for her work
exercise: The Ten Commandments say «You shall not steal». Which kind of dishonesty does this rule forbid? | Helping a friend | Telling a joke | Saying sorry | *Taking something that is not yours
exercise: Which of these is an example of dishonesty? | *Cheating by looking at someone else's test answers | Helping a friend carry bags | Saying thank you for a gift | Telling the truth about a broken vase
test: Dishonesty at work can lead to being sacked. What does sacked mean? | Paid extra money | Given a holiday | *Dismissed from your job | Promoted to a higher job
test: Proverbs 16:28 says, «A gossip separates close friends». Why might passing on a secret be a kind of dishonesty? | It helps close friends stay together | It makes friends laugh | It is always the same as telling the truth | *It breaks the trust that was given when the secret was shared
exam: Which is the best description of dishonesty? | A type of weather that is cold and wet | *Behaviour that is not honest, such as lying, cheating or stealing | Behaviour that is loud and noisy | Keeping a promise you made to a friend
